#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "1d103008-5f2b-56d3-bf46-9acb13be1e14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228039}] :question/id #uuid "7189602a-9b03-5e22-b3c9-dc1572c67ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332393" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$9\\frac{dy}{dx} + 18y = 4e^{2x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69474fc7-c867-5853-a468-3bbf94c86c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69474fc7-c867-5853-a468-3bbf94c86c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303095} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b2522e-10c4-500e-a4f6-6a85ec718f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de7735d2-3904-5b74-a25a-790295b495b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332398" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9b0e257f-3041-5c27-9229-515be1b893d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228041}] :question/id #uuid "5d6b3581-3918-5014-8781-891024445772" :question/math-academy-id "q-332426" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} - 6y = 8e^{2x}, y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 6y = 8e^{2x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c} = Ae^{6x}$, solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08eb864c-9ccf-5d1c-909b-0c8d0c9598fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08eb864c-9ccf-5d1c-909b-0c8d0c9598fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be460a3-3fac-5a9d-830b-8a6e65ea1408" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9119bbb7-05c6-5b96-825f-1b48a35651df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332531" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "2a0d502e-48ef-5a89-a581-fcf1fd3744f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228059}] :question/id #uuid "fcc20fdf-0331-56d0-9de8-00a5dcc17b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-332532" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 4y = 3e^{4x}$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{4x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "5a1c3645-f231-5b72-8d48-7f5f47936ca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228080}] :question/id #uuid "08f65c9c-ef2f-502a-89da-22e35becf913" :question/math-academy-id "q-332645" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$5\\frac{dy}{dx} + 10y = 15e^{-3x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7b7917-2ac5-5b52-a8e9-071c6e798cfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "036131be-8602-586a-8ee5-fe7271608cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-332723" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=6{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "39f4d02a-a086-55db-ba5c-b931f1ee1a3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228109}] :question/id #uuid "95f3da24-1dc4-5c26-969b-1c289b8f08a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-332797" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} - 3y = 9e^{3x}, y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 3y = 9e^{3x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c} = Ae^{3x}$, solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3df8082-e465-5116-a85f-68df826412e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3df8082-e465-5116-a85f-68df826412e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303657} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e98a4e4-c028-589a-8b58-7ac0d0bcae34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0a6279e-623c-5e5d-bfce-194a9b63b40e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332644" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=9{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{: