${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x})+14(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}) & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (-35+14)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ -21\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =21{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d5105ee-fe34-54db-8a0a-21ab91d763f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5105ee-fe34-54db-8a0a-21ab91d763f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303337} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c1a6e5-7995-5568-aa72-550a639d2bbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1545356e-4e65-553c-8118-fc733c23efcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-332667" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ (8\\alpha +8\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(12\\beta +8\\beta ){e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 16\\alpha {e}^{2x}+20\\beta {e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n16\\alpha =5,\\,\\,20\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{5}{16},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd84d653-9da0-52cf-9921-c75b5cb4683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd84d653-9da0-52cf-9921-c75b5cb4683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304048} :answer-field/id #uuid "42fbbd18-f58e-55c4-b2f1-ac390462f536" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ded7c26-5423-5b6f-b536-de9fa660d3cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332668" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x})+9(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}) & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3\\alpha +9\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(12\\beta +9\\beta ){e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 12\\alpha {e}^{x}+21\\beta {e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{4x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n12\\alpha =7\\,\\text{and}\\,21\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{7}{12}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{21}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa57c06-4f06-5e73-85e6-7ea49cfe4b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa57c06-4f06-5e73-85e6-7ea49cfe4b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303629} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cb2b8b4-f8fc-59fd-979c-5094044e61f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6adfa911-36fb-57ce-b523-2914b62bca69" :question/math-academy-id "q-332669" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ (2\\alpha +3\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(5\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x}+8\\beta {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{5x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n5\\alpha =2,\\,8\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e819727d-0c39-59a5-a1aa-de8b1e553484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e819727d-0c39-59a5-a1aa-de8b1e553484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303898} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d8de7e-fd86-518d-8a26-54633946daa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "945e99ed-3594-5be0-95b2-a2681c44f43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332670" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(4\\beta -6\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{x}-2\\beta {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{2x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha =3,\\,\\,-2\\beta =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e688a2-c92a-5042-bfbd-f0b603a09fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e688a2-c92a-5042-bfbd-f0b603a09fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302577} :answer-field/id #uuid "3232a174-1bc9-5e8d-b848-0b6f3565c118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9bf6129e-2981-5234-ae00-0e47f458eaf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332686" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+4\\alpha x{e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc428e2a-5a59-5fab-8d9f-a2c91fc34543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc428e2a-5a59-5fab-8d9f-a2c91fc34543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303714} :answer-field/id #uuid "152ee051-265b-51fd-9056-f2e48d9b7717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "332fe216-05e6-5450-9057-0e365b259ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332728" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd9cf94f-6d2c-5279-b476-55c7434eefef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303725} {:answer/id #uuid "06570ee4-ef32-50c5-80dd-d62ee7471554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06570ee4-ef32-50c5-80dd-d62ee7471554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425856} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37a43fd-f6c9-58a0-8e79-34180df6e0a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f0a9b7d-a8ca-55a3-9a38-83e9ea72a8f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332730" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e619bfcb-6971-5f4b-bd58-575535bb5ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e619bfcb-6971-5f4b-bd58-575535bb5ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303885} :answer-field/id #uuid "22eb4a61-85b8-5926-9377-3725744df7ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c1a6bf67-5f7d-58f8-b342-135abffd2994" :question/math-academy-id "q-332731" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=2{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{6x},$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x{e}^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5fd1e2-cc12-5558-a7ed-5ba45a7a9409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5fd1e2-cc12-5558-a7ed-5ba45a7a9409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303351} :answer-field/id #uuid "4016f4c8-a669-5084-abc5-0b4e5f44e38c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f4bcc57-6f0b-5c9c-9738-574f7a44b9ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-332795" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(2\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =10,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2380dec4-7275-5f6a-8b98-9506eb15d832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2380dec4-7275-5f6a-8b98-9506eb15d832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302681} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6e6bd2-253b-5a0c-abb7-063c121b89f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "732809e5-af22-52ea-a3ee-4e7666e27b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-332801" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=20{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $20{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c56bae-839a-5c23-84fc-41e84582c875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48c56bae-839a-5c23-84fc-41e84582c875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302914} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a2bfcbd-53ca-5ed0-9fa5-b2f15e9a8aa7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58b63a24-88b9-5660-8634-52a128aec0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332848" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =8,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c896b47-f7f4-50ef-bf8c-1e0ced4240c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30x{e}^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c896b47-f7f4-50ef-bf8c-1e0ced4240c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30x{e}^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302937} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0aef3a2-e9a1-5a1d-946c-79b52445d8ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bf7f4b1-c4f8-5e4d-98b4-df1be027b01f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332853" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x}+(\\beta +6\\beta ){e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x}+7\\beta {e}^{x} & =30{e}^{-6x}+4{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-6x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,7\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =30\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=30x{e}^{-6x}+\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b80934-34f0-5c6e-9245-6b3e7d29e95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b80934-34f0-5c6e-9245-6b3e7d29e95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304008} :answer-field/id #uuid "6015dde8-efa1-50fd-8036-aa2217f861bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37ac7932-4493-598d-be89-ff6da08a57c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332895" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7543fa00-9b05-5fa7-990f-d129855f76bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7543fa00-9b05-5fa7-990f-d129855f76bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303172} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f3085a-a862-52cd-aace-e26b3d972231" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28f09f6f-6042-518d-a393-24c52cb5f761" :question/math-academy-id "q-332898" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a8abd6-1b39-5703-bc81-72f3224b9093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a8abd6-1b39-5703-bc81-72f3224b9093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302884} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71dd12c-60df-5f92-bc42-c1396d7dc3c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c3810f2-6b8b-5c0c-b077-4ead6330565d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=6{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+6x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3ee253-ca25-54ae-9b3c-a2f91f225f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f3ee253-ca25-54ae-9b3c-a2f91f225f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302752} :answer-field/id #uuid "471edc8c-a0c1-58a8-ade1-0250fd08d091" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "763c9ce2-0497-58af-9158-f2e84fa94267" :question/math-academy-id "q-332903" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=6{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=6{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})-5(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2-5)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ -3\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2 & =4 \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e38272-0e4d-54de-9b6b-58fd2b9b2c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e38272-0e4d-54de-9b6b-58fd2b9b2c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303651} :answer-field/id #uuid "190fc58e-1f50-51f6-b37b-055cc67d42df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "edaf01e0-f315-5b89-a42e-b20571efeafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-332905" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{-x},$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-x}) & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ (-1-4)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1 & =-1 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f0126e4-e45f-57cd-83e7-308dc776bd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f0126e4-e45f-57cd-83e7-308dc776bd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302851} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eddb58f-ab47-553b-8044-663509e481aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2ab99a3-e4ae-5ac2-9c7d-f216d0d60347" :question/math-academy-id "q-332906" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=7{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=7{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-3\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{7}{5} & =0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003894d7-c1f0-5659-bf28-e65dae8b206d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "003894d7-c1f0-5659-bf28-e65dae8b206d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302447} :answer-field/id #uuid "116200ab-0cf3-5c69-9039-cd4a25757ae1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "516804e6-9850-5a07-aeb9-4a07986f8eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-332908" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=10\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{4x}-\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ 3\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=10$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =10 \\\\ A & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9bc9436-25c5-5166-bce0-48d389bf18f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9bc9436-25c5-5166-bce0-48d389bf18f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303604} :answer-field/id #uuid "0931645d-5e6e-519d-9c9d-4fa76d10d7b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e7dd865-a056-5710-ab6d-c5eb9bf72fc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332909" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =8 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0041758a-5182-50fb-a219-5d3f3ef02024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305153} {:answer/id #uuid "55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8x-1)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8x-1)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d58e7d-e179-5799-97c4-0c9c22e0553a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d63971f5-c416-5771-8c44-552fcfc50a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332936" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc5e30dc-a65d-53d9-80dc-c3a3790bad5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5e30dc-a65d-53d9-80dc-c3a3790bad5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307048} :answer-field/id #uuid "8126d16e-c4b7-5931-8387-6f268b8eee44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4df5e5b-65c9-5002-87ac-cf021a213a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-332937" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{4x}) & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca491ec8-db7c-5b57-b504-cd04e9792609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307030} {:answer/id #uuid "3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer/type {:db/id 