 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e64c2e40-5741-5178-904b-8ae1b402b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332726" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=9{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c009c47c-0010-58f0-af3f-4c58afce7657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c009c47c-0010-58f0-af3f-4c58afce7657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363744} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bffb413-d127-5384-b66a-53461a8d5549" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c0d25a5-20ac-54d3-b942-54173b2e140e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332942" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=7{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=7{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+x){e}^{x})-(\\alpha x{e}^{x}) & =7{e}^{x} \\\\ (1+x-x)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =7{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{x} & =7{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=7x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =4 \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf807d62-ca51-5961-a553-e83da1486686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf807d62-ca51-5961-a553-e83da1486686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363760} :answer-field/id #uuid "e122ceda-ade9-50d7-a34b-de3368ba119a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6cac7352-0dbf-5dae-970e-a3b99343c574" :question/math-academy-id "q-333174" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=4{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=4{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =4{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =4{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =4{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/math-academy-id "q-332708" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=9{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid 