& =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05391ed2-1af2-51c2-88ba-90a0af353d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05391ed2-1af2-51c2-88ba-90a0af353d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531842} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa06a60-3d8f-5b52-8f06-5733fb800f30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "011c879a-fc3f-5230-b160-60ca53316234" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′}+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-3(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+(2-3)\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x}-\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-1,\\qquad -\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-1,\\qquad \\beta =-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0-3 & =-1 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9518a01e-5e06-5c74-bbc4-71d8c76b9c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9518a01e-5e06-5c74-bbc4-71d8c76b9c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531852} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d5d707-9631-5fbb-954a-04917c307af2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "155bc372-bd60-53e4-b6c8-19b23fcc0aaa" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+6\\beta {e}^{6x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+6\\beta {e}^{6x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{6x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x}) & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(6+5)\\beta {e}^{6x} & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+11\\beta {e}^{6x} & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12,\\qquad 11\\beta =-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12,\\qquad \\beta =-\\frac{6}{11}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0-\\frac{6}{11} & =8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{94}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b8869f-a2ef-55c9-b294-a409bd69a909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91b8869f-a2ef-55c9-b294-a409bd69a909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531851} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e84de7c-53fc-5f01-bfdc-1ad4ad24d439" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "62226b80-0daa-5981-9200-5cc4183b2f0b" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}-\\beta {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}-\\beta {e}^{-x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-x}) & =-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(-1+3)\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-8,\\qquad 2\\beta =4.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-8,\\qquad \\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0+2 & =-4 \\\\ A & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e0410f9-5db5-5fda-8d88-144e5b3004c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e0410f9-5db5-5fda-8d88-144e5b3004c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531846} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf99e47-670f-558a-9e23-5e7fa9452cc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6f7b2bb6-e670-5a7b-8c29-a0c6dc5313b5" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. Neither exponential is a term in the complementary solution. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}-2\\beta {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}-2\\beta {e}^{-2x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x}) & =6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-5+3)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(-2+3)\\beta {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha =6,\\qquad \\beta =2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-3,\\qquad \\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3+2 & =4 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68609974-e2b8-547e-8dc8-ccf67124fc52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68609974-e2b8-547e-8dc8-ccf67124fc52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531848} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce5e95d-a2a1-5029-9a09-f5a50acc31f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a78885a6-4367-59e3-8a71-6384757be04b" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′}-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x})-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}-\\beta {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}-\\beta {e}^{-x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-x}) & =-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x}+(-1-5)\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x}-6\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-9,\\qquad -6\\beta =-10.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-9,\\qquad \\beta =\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0+\\frac{5}{3} & =4 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88b835c1-50c4-512d-bdc2-b620f146d449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673933} :answer-field/id #uuid "99caa933-48b2-5392-86f6-d8881556de6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6507ad75-48f6-531f-8654-44d77d1edbe5" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12{e}^{-6x},\\,y(0)=-7\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-6\\alpha {e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha {e}^{-6x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{-6x}) & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (-6-6)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ -12\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-7$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1 & =-7 \\\\ A & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afc1be2d-b249-5b7c-95cd-5254e3e2303d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673938} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d34ad3d-5eb8-561f-b712-48071750744d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=6{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=6{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})+5(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ (4+5)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 9\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =5 \\\\ A & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{13}{3}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3dfa19a-6690-5b37-a4d3-16184ec12210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673936} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aad149d-b776-594a-8b0f-dde71ca18723" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=10{e}^{3x},\\,y(0)=-6\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=10{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $10{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-4)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-10{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-6$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10 & =-6 \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{4x}-10{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50920f78-64a7-524d-a082-3e3c7fa50c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673926} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8c8fcd-18e1-59fa-bff5-93cd9b820b87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a79a19f5-81e1-5c0f-b705-f719ba52e841" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x})+4(\\alpha {e}^{-3x}) & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (-3+4)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{-4x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{57}{7}{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{57}{7}{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673928} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb13117-ae20-539e-b607-119478d2353a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3a814e3-11d3-5c6d-aded-548b37dd73b4" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=-8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is ${e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\alpha {e}^{5x})+2(\\alpha {e}^{5x}) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ (5+2)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ 7\\alpha {e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{1}{7} & =-8 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{57}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{57}{7}{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdbb6c1-e0b5-5b4d-a401-9a01b6f3feec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xe^x-3e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cdbb6c1-e0b5-5b4d-a401-9a01b6f3feec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xe^x-3e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724573} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3f79f3-0bdd-54d8-9ef4-4ca847b3a83c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01072c90-06fc-5d73-af82-65905f05fae1" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-y=4e^x+6e^{-x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^x$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Only $e^x$ occurs in the complementary solution; $e^{-x}$ does not. Multiply only the resonant trial by $x$ and take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha xe^x+\\beta e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nApply the product rule to $xe^x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha\\bigl((x)'e^x+x(e^x)'\\bigr)+\\beta(e^{-x})'\\\\\n&=\\alpha(e^x+xe^x)-\\beta e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha(1+x)e^x-\\beta e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute and collect the two distinct exponential terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'-y_p&=\\alpha(1+x)e^x-\\beta e^{-x}-(\\alpha xe^x+\\beta e^{-x})\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^x-2\\beta e^{-x}\\\\\n&=4e^x+6e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients gives $\\alpha=4$ and $-2\\beta=6$, so $\\beta=-3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=4xe^x-3e^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4828edb8-23ea-54e9-b26e-61bfaddb9676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^x+4xe^{-2x}-4e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4828edb8-23ea-54e9-b26e-61bfaddb9676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^x+4xe^{-2x}-4e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724570} :answer-field/id #uuid "593c7d1c-3da6-525f-a423-a75b7dd0ec0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a211b61-79fd-56d6-aa0e-21e89285eaa7" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+2y=6e^x+4e^{-2x}-4e^{-x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{-2x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compare each forcing exponential with the complementary solution. Only $e^{-2x}$ occurs there, so only its trial needs a factor of $x$. Use three unknown coefficients:\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^x+\\beta xe^{-2x}+\\gamma e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha e^x+\\beta\\bigl((x)'e^{-2x}+x(e^{-2x})'\\bigr)-\\gamma e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^x+\\beta(e^{-2x}-2xe^{-2x})-\\gamma e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^x+\\beta(1-2x)e^{-2x}-\\gamma e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the equation and collect each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'+2y_p&=\\alpha e^x+\\beta(1-2x)e^{-2x}-\\gamma e^{-x}\\\\\n&\\quad+2(\\alpha e^x+\\beta xe^{-2x}+\\gamma e^{-x})\\\\\n&=3\\alpha e^x+\\beta e^{-2x}+\\gamma e^{-x}\\\\\n&=6e^x+4e^{-2x}-4e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^x$, $e^{-2x}$, and $e^{-x}$ gives\n\n$$\n3\\alpha=6,\\qquad\\beta=4,\\qquad\\gamma=-4.\n$$\n\nThus $\\alpha=2$, $\\beta=4$, and $\\gamma=-4$. A particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=2e^x+4xe^{-2x}-4e^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e74c4ab-3648-54f3-9261-3ff76d17c44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e74c4ab-3648-54f3-9261-3ff76d17c44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724568} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b16a42-f007-5fab-ac3f-19a32bcd8f00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea684c78-5fdf-5148-805f-59e8555b2cb1" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+2y=5e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{-2x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{-2x}$ already occurs in the complementary solution. A trial $\\alpha e^{-2x}$ would give zero on the left, so multiply this trial by $x$:\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x e^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha\\bigl((x)'e^{-2x}+x(e^{-2x})'\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\alpha(e^{-2x}-2xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=\\alpha(1-2x)e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'+2y_p&=\\alpha(1-2x)e^{-2x}+2\\alpha xe^{-2x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^{-2x}=5e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients gives $\\alpha=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=5xe^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a38e7e42-4cfd-5cf4-b5a3-15e0d4160a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}-4xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a38e7e42-4cfd-5cf4-b5a3-15e0d4160a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}-4xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724598} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9e52ed-b985-5fe1-b797-eacbe4610e82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3408b6d0-48c2-53a7-a35d-ee28d1c578da" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+3y=-4e^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ae^{-3x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Use $A$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution is $y=y_c+y_p$. The supplied complementary solution solves the associated homogeneous equation; we must find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\nThe forcing exponential $e^{-3x}$ already occurs in $y_c$. A trial $\\alpha e^{-3x}$ would give zero on the left-hand side. Multiply it by $x$ and try\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha xe^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nThe product rule gives\n\n$$\ny_p'=\\alpha e^{-3x}-3\\alpha xe^{-3x}=\\alpha(1-3x)e^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstitution produces the cancellation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_p'+3y_p&=\\alpha(1-3x)e^{-3x}+3\\alpha xe^{-3x}\\\\&=\\alpha e^{-3x}=-4e^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $\\alpha=-4$ and $y_p=-4xe^{-3x}$. The general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=Ae^{-3x}-4xe^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nThe $x$ factor is essential: a trial without it would contribute zero to $y_p'+3y_p$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "518bcd25-524a-5e53-bc75-7fe189399387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^x+3xe^x+2e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "518bcd25-524a-5e53-bc75-7fe189399387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^x+3xe^x+2e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724594} :answer-field/id #uuid "74dc9269-b2ab-5ddc-93d9-a742d8ef3d7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "39f45926-cbe8-53b9-99f5-41f4f4bb9db2" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-y=3e^x-4e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ae^x$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Use $A$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution is $y=y_c+y_p$. The supplied complementary solution solves the associated homogeneous equation; we must find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\nTreat the two forcing terms separately. The exponential $e^x$ occurs in $y_c=Ae^x$, so its trial needs an $x$ factor. The exponential $e^{-x}$ does not occur in $y_c$, so its trial needs no such factor. Use\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha xe^x+\\beta e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with the product rule gives\n\n$$\ny_p'=\\alpha(1+x)e^x-\\beta e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and collect each exponential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_p'-y_p&=\\alpha(1+x)e^x-\\beta e^{-x}-(\\alpha xe^x+\\beta e^{-x})\\\\&=\\alpha e^x-2\\beta e^{-x}\\\\&=3e^x-4e^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the distinct exponentials gives $\\alpha=3$ and $-2\\beta=-4$, so $\\beta=2$. Thus $y_p=3xe^x+2e^{-x}$. Adding $y_c$ gives\n\n$$\ny(x)=Ae^x+3xe^x+2e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFor a direct check, $(3xe^x)\\prime-3xe^x=3e^x$ and $(2e^{-x})\\prime-2e^{-x}=-4e^{-x}$. Their sum supplies exactly the required forcing."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81605c17-6000-5d56-80e4-bdd02d74f762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-2x}+2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81605c17-6000-5d56-80e4-bdd02d74f762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-2x}+2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724597} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e0194a-c334-5a5b-9a71-69edd14bf4bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5590e8d9-4abc-53c5-b223-3d77a3234374" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+2y=6e^x.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Use $A$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution is $y=y_c+y_p$. The supplied complementary solution solves the associated homogeneous equation; we must find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\nThe forcing exponential $e^x$ does not occur in $y_c=Ae^{-2x}$. Try $y_p=\\alpha e^x$, where $\\alpha$ is a constant. Differentiating gives $y_p'=\\alpha e^x$.\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_p'+2y_p&=\\alpha e^x+2\\alpha e^x\\\\&=3\\alpha e^x=6e^x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $3\\alpha=6$, so $\\alpha=2$ and $y_p=2e^x$. Adding the complementary solution gives\n\n$$\ny(x)=Ae^{-2x}+2e^x.\n$$\n\nAs a check, $(2e^x)\\prime+2(2e^x)=6e^x$, and $(Ae^{-2x})\\prime+2Ae^{-2x}=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724638 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0679a3b-6007-59f8-b405-0c741c7c915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}{e}^{5x}+\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0679a3b-6007-59f8-b405-0c741c7c915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}{e}^{5x}+\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724644} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec505e5-c487-56fb-9f6d-a6a08a640a5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "412b68a5-b2de-54b0-9e61-0b29fc6596fd" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=5{e}^{5x}-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{5x}-5{e}^{-3x},$ and neither ${e}^{5x}$ nor ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-3\\beta {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(5\\alpha )-6\\alpha ){e}^{5x} &= 5{e}^{5x} \\\\ (3(-3\\beta )-6\\beta ){e}^{-3x} &= -5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{5x}$, ${e}^{-3x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n9\\alpha =5\\,\\text{and}\\,-15\\beta =-5,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{5}{9}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{5}{9} = \\frac{5}{9} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{-5}{-15} = \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{9}{e}^{5x}+\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d89d95-f71d-53a2-afb6-38a9c8ffd60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1d89d95-f71d-53a2-afb6-38a9c8ffd60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724649} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aeea7c2-83f0-5d19-b020-9c221fe1027d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73f53169-83dc-5e2b-9294-6015cef182e8" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=4{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{-3x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(-3\\alpha )-9\\alpha ){e}^{-3x} &= 4{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (3(2\\beta )-9\\beta ){e}^{2x} &= {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$, ${e}^{2x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-18\\alpha =4\\,\\text{and}\\,-3\\beta =1,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{4}{-18} = -\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{1}{-3} = -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504e683e-dd12-5f52-81a7-7ef45f08502b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504e683e-dd12-5f52-81a7-7ef45f08502b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde7e1b9-c2b1-5b5e-81e4-358c19925118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e839ee1b-ac4e-5b97-a288-96c6d5a9e19f" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-6{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-6{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-4x},$ and neither ${e}^{-x}$ nor ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}+\\beta {e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}-4\\beta {e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(-\\alpha )+6\\alpha ){e}^{-x} &= -6{e}^{-x} \\\\ (3(-4\\beta )+6\\beta ){e}^{-4x} &= {e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-x}$, ${e}^{-4x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n3\\alpha =-6\\,\\text{and}\\,-6\\beta =1,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-2\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{-6}{3} = -2 \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{1}{-6} = -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115bf239-ba75-5d49-bc5d-de3b67cbcb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{4}{15}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "115bf239-ba75-5d49-bc5d-de3b67cbcb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{4}{15}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724636} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfdc8a1-4daf-5920-93e9-c069fd1dd50a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee4d440f-ec6f-54be-a0a0-ea192610ba79" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=-{e}^{-3x}-4{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-{e}^{-3x}-4{e}^{4x},$ and neither ${e}^{-3x}$ nor ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(-3\\alpha )+3\\alpha ){e}^{-3x} &= -{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (3(4\\beta )+3\\beta ){e}^{4x} &= -4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$, ${e}^{4x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha =-1\\,\\text{and}\\,15\\beta =-4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-\\frac{4}{15}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{-1}{-6} = \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{-4}{15} = -\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{4}{15}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e134500f-cf17-5c8f-9b3d-347e3c47dc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e134500f-cf17-5c8f-9b3d-347e3c47dc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724645} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d27760-8e42-55a8-b9fc-b13f0ed95684" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "efbf34f1-f193-5632-b798-079280505168" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{3x},$ and neither ${e}^{-5x}$ nor ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2(-5\\alpha )-2\\alpha ){e}^{-5x} &= 4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (2(3\\beta )-2\\beta ){e}^{3x} &= -2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-5x}$, ${e}^{3x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha =4\\,\\text{and}\\,4\\beta =-2,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{4}{-12} = -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{-2}{4} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e003b532-a597-5127-9df9-cda24bb6e0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{4x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e003b532-a597-5127-9df9-cda24bb6e0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{4x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724660} :answer-field/id #uuid "042a6c81-95ad-58b9-93fc-9264f0bfd882" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1eae118e-6a83-51af-a8ea-c8404bddd575" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{4x}+3{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{4x}+3{e}^{2x},$ and none of these exponentials occurs in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}+\\gamma {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}+2\\gamma {e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nTo find these constants, we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(-5\\alpha )-3\\alpha ){e}^{-5x} &= 5{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (3(4\\beta )-3\\beta ){e}^{4x} &= -{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3(2\\gamma )-3\\gamma ){e}^{2x} &= 3{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-5x}$, ${e}^{4x}$, ${e}^{2x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-18\\alpha =5,\\quad 9\\beta =-1,\\quad 3\\gamma =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{18},\\quad \\beta =-\\frac{1}{9},\\quad \\gamma =1.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{5}{-18} = -\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{-1}{9} = -\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ \\gamma  &= \\frac{3}{3} = 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{4x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fe31cd-f984-5a43-b042-a45e219fc1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37fe31cd-f984-5a43-b042-a45e219fc1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0f197f3-80c5-52ec-aff9-020d76f0ee68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "476d5812-8160-50e4-98b1-54b7e09c323e" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=4{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{4x}-4{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{4x}-4{e}^{-5x},$ and none of these exponentials occurs in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}+\\gamma {e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}-5\\gamma {e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nTo find these constants, we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(2\\alpha )-9\\alpha ){e}^{2x} &= 4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (3(4\\beta )-9\\beta ){e}^{4x} &= 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3(-5\\gamma )-9\\gamma ){e}^{-5x} &= -4{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$, ${e}^{4x}$, ${e}^{-5x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha =4,\\quad 3\\beta =4,\\quad -24\\gamma =-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{3},\\quad \\beta =\\frac{4}{3},\\quad \\gamma =\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{4}{-3} = -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\gamma  &= \\frac{-4}{-24} = \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e645de8e-f0f4-57ec-92ec-3f9d7cb83234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e645de8e-f0f4-57ec-92ec-3f9d7cb83234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724661} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f5fd54-f408-5fe5-bcc2-98766314032f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6ee5d9df-5146-5bae-b2fd-0cdefb4bf4c7" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=-2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{3x},$ and none of these exponentials occurs in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}+\\gamma {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}+3\\gamma {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find these constants, we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3(-\\alpha )-3\\alpha ){e}^{-x} &= -2{e}^{-x} \\\\ (3(2\\beta )-3\\beta ){e}^{2x} &= -{e}^{2x} \\\\ (3(3\\gamma )-3\\gamma ){e}^{3x} &= -2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-x}$, ${e}^{2x}$, ${e}^{3x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha =-2,\\quad 3\\beta =-1,\\quad 6\\gamma =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3},\\quad \\beta =-\\frac{1}{3},\\quad \\gamma =-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{-2}{-6} = \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{-1}{3} = -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\gamma  &= \\frac{-2}{6} = -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7908e145-2918-558f-aa8d-ef724fce781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7908e145-2918-558f-aa8d-ef724fce781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724658} :answer-field/id #uuid "57cb21dc-9d16-5b68-9c09-562cc06bfee7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f1d205d0-6b0a-54d7-be72-3bbc29acf80f" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=3{e}^{4x}+5{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{4x}+5{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{3x},$ and none of these exponentials occurs in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x}+\\beta {e}^{-5x}+\\gamma {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}-5\\beta {e}^{-5x}+3\\gamma {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find these constants, we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4\\alpha )+3\\alpha ){e}^{4x} &= 3{e}^{4x} \\\\ ((-5\\beta )+3\\beta ){e}^{-5x} &= 5{e}^{-5x} \\\\ ((3\\gamma )+3\\gamma ){e}^{3x} &= -2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{4x}$, ${e}^{-5x}$, ${e}^{3x}$, respectively, gives\n\n$$\n7\\alpha =3,\\quad -2\\beta =5,\\quad 6\\gamma =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{3}{7},\\quad \\beta =-\\frac{5}{2},\\quad \\gamma =-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{3}{7} = \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\beta  &= \\frac{5}{-2} = -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\gamma  &= \\frac{-2}{6} = -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{7}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "262675b1-8705-5351-a7cc-83671da57c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262675b1-8705-5351-a7cc-83671da57c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724664} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39d8bdf-ac12-59cc-9b85-831d0ad4c00d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f945d601-ccc8-544b-b8d1-00f3121a4404" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=-{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. Express your answer using only the exponentials in the forcing term.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation and group each exponential separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((-3\\alpha )+2\\alpha ){e}^{-3x} &= -{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving each coefficient equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  &= \\frac{-1}{-1} = 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "798883b3-b060-5240-abc7-357186ab1c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}e^{-x}+8xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798883b3-b060-5240-abc7-357186ab1c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}e^{-x}+8xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724673} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60b6886-dc1f-5d3a-acfd-005d3d387d8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ae6b09c-2902-512a-96a6-a7d164a795d4" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=11e^{-x}+8e^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{2x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{2x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta xe^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (e^{-x})'+\\beta (xe^{2x})'\\\\\n&=-\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta (e^{2x}+2xe^{2x})\\\\\n&=-\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta (1+2x)e^{2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'-2y_p&=(-\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta (1+2x)e^{2x})\\\\\n&\\quad -2(\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta xe^{2x})\\\\\n&=(-\\alpha -2\\alpha )e^{-x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1+2x-2x)\\beta e^{2x}\\\\\n&=-3\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta e^{2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $11e^{-x}+8e^{2x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha =11,\\qquad \\beta =8.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{11}{3},\\qquad \\beta =8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-\\frac{11}{3}e^{-x}+8xe^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcef0320-c7e1-5ed0-a02c-085af2127fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5xe^{5x}+6e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcef0320-c7e1-5ed0-a02c-085af2127fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5xe^{5x}+6e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724679} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ab16b8-5520-5a72-83e1-c2761a88504e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72199593-3cc7-5ddb-98e7-581a78b0220d" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-5y=-5e^{5x}+6e^{6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{5x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{5x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha xe^{5x}+\\beta e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (xe^{5x})'+\\beta (e^{6x})'\\\\\n&=\\alpha (e^{5x}+5xe^{5x})+6\\beta e^{6x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha (1+5x)e^{5x}+6\\beta e^{6x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'-5y_p&=(\\alpha (1+5x)e^{5x}+6\\beta e^{6x})\\\\\n&\\quad -5(\\alpha xe^{5x}+\\beta e^{6x})\\\\\n&=(1+5x-5x)\\alpha e^{5x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(6\\beta -5\\beta )e^{6x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^{5x}+\\beta e^{6x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $-5e^{5x}+6e^{6x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-5,\\qquad \\beta =6.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-5,\\qquad \\beta =6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-5xe^{5x}+6e^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d029c98-4265-5098-9e13-5d4967b25027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-4x}+4xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d029c98-4265-5098-9e13-5d4967b25027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-4x}+4xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724668} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6c406a-4bf9-5fbb-aeae-5f7fb5b9b5f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a52201a4-e3ed-52fa-b4b5-d24247672f23" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+2y=-4e^{-4x}+4e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{-2x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{-2x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^{-4x}+\\beta xe^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (e^{-4x})'+\\beta (xe^{-2x})'\\\\\n&=-4\\alpha e^{-4x}+\\beta (e^{-2x}-2xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=-4\\alpha e^{-4x}+\\beta (1-2x)e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'+2y_p&=(-4\\alpha e^{-4x}+\\beta (1-2x)e^{-2x})\\\\\n&\\quad +2(\\alpha e^{-4x}+\\beta xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=(-4\\alpha +2\\alpha )e^{-4x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1-2x+2x)\\beta e^{-2x}\\\\\n&=-2\\alpha e^{-4x}+\\beta e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $-4e^{-4x}+4e^{-2x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha =-4,\\qquad \\beta =4.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =2,\\qquad \\beta =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=2e^{-4x}+4xe^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e2ec390-0cd0-5bac-9fad-4d30e6fa4764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{-x}-12xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e2ec390-0cd0-5bac-9fad-4d30e6fa4764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{-x}-12xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724671} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16ddb50-b5a8-5bde-bbb2-1b6f3aa9de92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4aad45e-da85-5952-b570-dcfbf7ef9f27" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+2y=-3e^{-x}-12e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{-2x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{-2x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta xe^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (e^{-x})'+\\beta (xe^{-2x})'\\\\\n&=-\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta (e^{-2x}-2xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=-\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta (1-2x)e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'+2y_p&=(-\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta (1-2x)e^{-2x})\\\\\n&\\quad +2(\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=(-\\alpha +2\\alpha )e^{-x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1-2x+2x)\\beta e^{-2x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^{-x}+\\beta e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $-3e^{-x}-12e^{-2x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-3,\\qquad \\beta =-12.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-3,\\qquad \\beta =-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-3e^{-x}-12xe^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d57eab83-161f-52eb-a0d9-94fbd4995dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7xe^{-4x}+\\frac{11}{3}e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d57eab83-161f-52eb-a0d9-94fbd4995dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7xe^{-4x}+\\frac{11}{3}e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724678} :answer-field/id #uuid "dedf047e-9637-52f0-adef-713218bf21a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e4756831-e40d-5475-a596-abbcff939b5d" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+4y=-7e^{-4x}+11e^{-x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{-4x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{-4x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha xe^{-4x}+\\beta e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (xe^{-4x})'+\\beta (e^{-x})'\\\\\n&=\\alpha (e^{-4x}-4xe^{-4x})-\\beta e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha (1-4x)e^{-4x}-\\beta e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'+4y_p&=(\\alpha (1-4x)e^{-4x}-\\beta e^{-x})\\\\\n&\\quad +4(\\alpha xe^{-4x}+\\beta e^{-x})\\\\\n&=(1-4x+4x)\\alpha e^{-4x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-\\beta +4\\beta )e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^{-4x}+3\\beta e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $-7e^{-4x}+11e^{-x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-7,\\qquad 3\\beta =11.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-7,\\qquad \\beta =\\frac{11}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-7xe^{-4x}+\\frac{11}{3}e^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55c8e9f2-f181-5852-8114-60ce6591bac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e^{-2x}+4xe^{x}+4e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55c8e9f2-f181-5852-8114-60ce6591bac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e^{-2x}+4xe^{x}+4e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724685} :answer-field/id #uuid "f61b4d42-2cac-5e73-946a-6054d68b744c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3240b86f-71ad-5f64-a106-fd20de6124d1" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-y=12e^{-2x}+4e^{x}-8e^{-x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta xe^{x}+\\gamma e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (e^{-2x})'+\\beta (xe^{x})'+\\gamma (e^{-x})'\\\\\n&=-2\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta (e^{x}+xe^{x})-\\gamma e^{-x}\\\\\n&=-2\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta (1+x)e^{x}-\\gamma e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'-y_p&=(-2\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta (1+x)e^{x}-\\gamma e^{-x})\\\\\n&\\quad -(\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta xe^{x}+\\gamma e^{-x})\\\\\n&=(-2\\alpha -\\alpha )e^{-2x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1+x-x)\\beta e^{x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-\\gamma -\\gamma )e^{-x}\\\\\n&=-3\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta e^{x}-2\\gamma e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $12e^{-2x}+4e^{x}-8e^{-x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha =12,\\qquad \\beta =4,\\qquad -2\\gamma =-8.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-4,\\qquad \\beta =4,\\qquad \\gamma =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-4e^{-2x}+4xe^{x}+4e^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd39a18f-918a-5417-ad95-205a2f76b839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xe^{x}-3e^{3x}-4e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd39a18f-918a-5417-ad95-205a2f76b839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xe^{x}-3e^{3x}-4e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724694} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a34ab8c-258a-5ca8-8434-3fe7a75844ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5757edd6-cc74-5dd6-b2fb-9ab748ea9505" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-y=-4e^{x}-6e^{3x}+8e^{-x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha xe^{x}+\\beta e^{3x}+\\gamma e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (xe^{x})'+\\beta (e^{3x})'+\\gamma (e^{-x})'\\\\\n&=\\alpha (e^{x}+xe^{x})+3\\beta e^{3x}-\\gamma e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha (1+x)e^{x}+3\\beta e^{3x}-\\gamma e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'-y_p&=(\\alpha (1+x)e^{x}+3\\beta e^{3x}-\\gamma e^{-x})\\\\\n&\\quad -(\\alpha xe^{x}+\\beta e^{3x}+\\gamma e^{-x})\\\\\n&=(1+x-x)\\alpha e^{x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(3\\beta -\\beta )e^{3x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-\\gamma -\\gamma )e^{-x}\\\\\n&=\\alpha e^{x}+2\\beta e^{3x}-2\\gamma e^{-x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $-4e^{x}-6e^{3x}+8e^{-x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-4,\\qquad 2\\beta =-6,\\qquad -2\\gamma =8.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-4,\\qquad \\beta =-3,\\qquad \\gamma =-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-4xe^{x}-3e^{3x}-4e^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee643de-e0f7-547b-b08e-ee58864d10f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{2x}-e^{x}+4xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ee643de-e0f7-547b-b08e-ee58864d10f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{2x}-e^{x}+4xe^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724689} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d61129-bd61-5935-9d70-557a5ad4ee5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "67b79eb3-fe63-5c60-8650-2b24a4945d1a" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'+2y=-12e^{2x}-3e^{x}+4e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{-2x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{-2x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^{2x}+\\beta e^{x}+\\gamma xe^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (e^{2x})'+\\beta (e^{x})'+\\gamma (xe^{-2x})'\\\\\n&=2\\alpha e^{2x}+\\beta e^{x}+\\gamma (e^{-2x}-2xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=2\\alpha e^{2x}+\\beta e^{x}+\\gamma (1-2x)e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'+2y_p&=(2\\alpha e^{2x}+\\beta e^{x}+\\gamma (1-2x)e^{-2x})\\\\\n&\\quad +2(\\alpha e^{2x}+\\beta e^{x}+\\gamma xe^{-2x})\\\\\n&=(2\\alpha +2\\alpha )e^{2x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(\\beta +2\\beta )e^{x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1-2x+2x)\\gamma e^{-2x}\\\\\n&=4\\alpha e^{2x}+3\\beta e^{x}+\\gamma e^{-2x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $-12e^{2x}-3e^{x}+4e^{-2x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n4\\alpha =-12,\\qquad 3\\beta =-3,\\qquad \\gamma =4.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-3,\\qquad \\beta =-1,\\qquad \\gamma =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-3e^{2x}-e^{x}+4xe^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dbbf40c-fe95-5b4a-b258-87f0941f0f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2x}-e^{-3x}+4xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dbbf40c-fe95-5b4a-b258-87f0941f0f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2x}-e^{-3x}+4xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724688} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ae64ee-99ec-5da9-b353-ea12cb80e0b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a9e91bb6-56f8-56ea-8d55-e2880c4ddadb" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-3y=10e^{-2x}+6e^{-3x}+4e^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x)=Ae^{3x}$, find a particular solution. Do not add a multiple of the complementary solution.\n\n$y_p(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The exponential $e^{3x}$ occurs in the complementary solution. An unmodified trial for that mode would give zero on the left, so multiply only that trial by $x$. The other forcing exponentials do not occur in the complementary solution. Take\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta e^{-3x}+\\gamma xe^{3x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate, using the product rule on the resonant term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'&=\\alpha (e^{-2x})'+\\beta (e^{-3x})'+\\gamma (xe^{3x})'\\\\\n&=-2\\alpha e^{-2x}-3\\beta e^{-3x}+\\gamma (e^{3x}+3xe^{3x})\\\\\n&=-2\\alpha e^{-2x}-3\\beta e^{-3x}+\\gamma (1+3x)e^{3x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the differential equation and collect like exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_p'-3y_p&=(-2\\alpha e^{-2x}-3\\beta e^{-3x}+\\gamma (1+3x)e^{3x})\\\\\n&\\quad -3(\\alpha e^{-2x}+\\beta e^{-3x}+\\gamma xe^{3x})\\\\\n&=(-2\\alpha -3\\alpha )e^{-2x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(-3\\beta -3\\beta )e^{-3x}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1+3x-3x)\\gamma e^{3x}\\\\\n&=-5\\alpha e^{-2x}-6\\beta e^{-3x}+\\gamma e^{3x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis must equal $10e^{-2x}+6e^{-3x}+4e^{3x}$. Equating the coefficient of each distinct exponential gives\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha =10,\\qquad -6\\beta =6,\\qquad \\gamma =4.\n$$\n\nSolving these coefficient equations,\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-2,\\qquad \\beta =-1,\\qquad \\gamma =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=-2e^{-2x}-e^{-3x}+4xe^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e11cf177-e39a-522a-acf8-8b53fa0ca1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}-4{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e11cf177-e39a-522a-acf8-8b53fa0ca1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}-4{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724729} :answer-field/id #uuid "313a6dfe-a413-5cb6-9772-2de286a92b77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "02aa8dbe-863f-5844-acb1-58a4d683a0ed" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+5y=-4{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-5x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-4{e}^{-4x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{\\prime}=-4\\alpha {e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}-4{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dbae862-5f59-5e7b-a055-ca53c42e6427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}+4{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dbae862-5f59-5e7b-a055-ca53c42e6427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}+4{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724722} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc849455-6bf2-5e83-bb80-8d24fd96c8ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c7bb9dc-f17d-582a-b522-a842b07ef9ef" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+4y=40{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-4x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $40{e}^{6x},$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{\\prime}=6\\alpha {e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha {e}^{6x}+4\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =40{e}^{6x} \\\\ 10\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =40{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+4{e}^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb05e8e7-5810-5dfc-891a-526b6dd98c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}-5{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb05e8e7-5810-5dfc-891a-526b6dd98c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-2x}-5{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724730} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d45feed-f167-574d-999c-7787486d3e79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "383c80f8-2422-54e7-9219-1be291f2f627" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+2y=-20{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-2x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-20{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{\\prime}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+2\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-20{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}-5{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95947494-9664-57ec-bf21-ebb435f0a09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95947494-9664-57ec-bf21-ebb435f0a09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724727} :answer-field/id #uuid "79771c10-b2ee-5043-be8b-561620ad8ef6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50aea0fd-8dbc-5780-a798-35e628ca1596" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+y=-2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-2{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{\\prime}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\alpha {e}^{x} & =-2{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =-2{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e771113-d503-57c1-9fdd-22e9b0405e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e771113-d503-57c1-9fdd-22e9b0405e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724726} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aeffdd4-7367-5c9e-8769-6cf9c7c04fb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bac1844-0953-55d5-9d17-defc3bbcf608" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}-3y=3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{3x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{\\prime}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-3\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f9d8c68-6df5-5263-833f-75cb2ef02aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f9d8c68-6df5-5263-833f-75cb2ef02aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724733} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4e997b-a2d5-577c-8600-ab2d7d717679" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5509cce7-9aaa-5e7e-b5e8-024867ac1dae" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}-3y=3{e}^{3x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{3x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{3x}+{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{\\prime} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{\\prime}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{\\prime}-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =\\alpha {e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\beta {e}^{2x})^{\\prime}-3\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{\\prime}-3{y}_{p} & =\\alpha {e}^{3x}-\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum must equal the forcing in the question. Equating coefficients of ${e}^{3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -\\beta=1,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{3x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79dac299-3f20-5343-a3cc-5d9f4b31b092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}-3x{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79dac299-3f20-5343-a3cc-5d9f4b31b092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}-3x{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724736} :answer-field/id #uuid "8518ccaa-e1f7-501e-9ef4-443ad09a0d49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88b4c5ba-989a-585a-bdb7-aa65344ae8fb" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+5y=-14{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-5x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-14{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{-5x},$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{\\prime} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{\\prime}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{\\prime}+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =\\alpha {e}^{-5x} \\\\ (\\beta {e}^{2x})^{\\prime}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =7\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{\\prime}+5{y}_{p} & =\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+7\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum must equal the forcing in the question. Equating coefficients of ${e}^{-5x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 7\\beta=-14,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}-3x{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f6a286-ef15-5347-a2ab-c4b6e0a25f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f6a286-ef15-5347-a2ab-c4b6e0a25f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724734} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd4fd34-e470-512e-98e6-5479077f7b5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e64afc8f-397a-51fa-9b83-cdb5046598ad" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+3y=6{e}^{-5x}-4{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-3x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-5x}-4{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{-5x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{\\prime} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-5x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{\\prime}+\\beta ({e}^{-5x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})-5\\beta {e}^{-5x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}-5\\beta {e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{\\prime}+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =\\alpha {e}^{-3x} \\\\ (\\beta {e}^{-5x})^{\\prime}+3\\beta {e}^{-5x} & =-2\\beta {e}^{-5x} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{\\prime}+3{y}_{p} & =\\alpha {e}^{-3x}-2\\beta {e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum must equal the forcing in the question. Equating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -2\\beta=6,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4x{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2ae4141-3236-543f-b275-ef9b5571a1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+5x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2ae4141-3236-543f-b275-ef9b5571a1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+5x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724741} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f6e1838-713d-5ed5-adc7-bde85ee63139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7060007-e655-5dc4-840d-85f45198bd1c" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\\prime}+y=2{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWrite the arbitrary constant term as $A{e}^{-x}$.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{\\prime}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-x},$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{-2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{\\prime} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-x})^{\\prime}+\\beta ({e}^{-2x})^{\\prime} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})-2\\beta {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x}-2\\beta {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\\prime}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha x{e}^{-x})^{\\prime}+\\alpha x{e}^{-x} & =\\alpha {e}^{-x} \\\\ (\\beta {e}^{-2x})^{\\prime}+\\beta {e}^{-2x} & =-\\beta {e}^{-2x} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{\\prime}+{y}_{p} & =\\alpha {e}^{-x}-\\beta {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum must equal the forcing in the question. Equating coefficients of ${e}^{-x}$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -\\beta=2,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+5x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}]}
